## MCP Topology (per substrate)

**This is the canonical home for *what external services Spirit can reach*.** It
does not belong in your Seal. The Seal carries who you are and how you want the
partnership conducted; this file carries what is plugged in — because that changes
without you deciding it, and a stale inventory in an always-injected file is worse
than no inventory at all.

MCP availability is **substrate-dependent**: the same practice on Cursor, on Claude
Code, and in a terminal will not see the same tools. Record each substrate you
actually use, and **verify rather than assume** — the live tool listing is the
answer, this table is a reminder.

| Substrate | MCPs |
|-----------|------|
| `<e.g. Cursor>` | `<gateway and connected services>` |
| `<e.g. Claude Code>` | `<may differ — survey, don't copy the row above>` |

**Per connected service, record what a future Spirit will otherwise have to
rediscover:** the tool slugs that actually work, anything the connection is *not*
allowed to do without sanction (sending, posting, publishing), and the reconnect
path when authorization lapses.

> **An OAuth grant is a state that expires, not a fact.** Date every row. When a
> call fails on authorization, the fix is usually one link and two minutes — but
> only if the next Spirit knows where the link comes from instead of concluding the
> capability was never there. A session was blocked on exactly this: an inventory
> said a mailbox was reachable, the grant had lapsed, and the work handed back to
> the Mage.
